            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Slides a kernel - a short list of weights - along the values, and at each position adds up the values under it, each times its weight. A kernel of equal weights averages (smoothing); a bell-shaped one blurs gently; 1 -1 gives the change from one value to the next. Where the input has a single spike, a copy of the kernel appears: convolving places the kernel's shape at every impulse.\n\nThis is true convolution, as np.convolve does it (the kernel is applied reversed). For symmetric kernels that makes no difference; torch's own conv1d correlates, as t.correlate does.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.convolve <kernel values ...> <mode>\nThe kernel can be given here: t.convolve 1 2 1. The mode can also be given: same, full\nor valid.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe values. Every input sends.\n\nkernel:\nThe weights, held.\n\ndim (int box, also an inlet):\nThe dim the kernel slides along; -1, the last, by default. For frames x joints, use 0.\n\nmode:\nAn option, under the node. same (the default) keeps the input's length; full includes every position where the kernel overlaps the input at all (length + kernel - 1); valid only the positions where it overlaps completely.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nconvolved:\nThe result.\n\nThe answer stays on the input's device: a tensor on the GPU is worked on there and comes out there.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTwo spikes, convolved with the kernel 1 2 1: each spike becomes a copy of the kernel.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.convolve is the numpy version. t.correlate finds where a pattern occurs. scipy.filtfilt and the Butterworth filters smooth by frequency. scipy.gaussian_filter blurs.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 1300,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Slides a kernel - a short list of weights - along the values, and at each position adds up the values under it, each times its weight. A kernel of equal weights averages (smoothing); a bell-shaped one blurs gently; 1 -1 gives the change from one value to the next. Where the input has a single spike, a copy of the kernel appears: convolving places the kernel's shape at every impulse.\n\nThis is true convolution, as np.convolve does it (the kernel is applied reversed). For symmetric kernels that makes no difference; torch's own conv1d correlates, as t.correlate does.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.convolve <kernel values ...> <mode>\nThe kernel can be given here: t.convolve 1 2 1. The mode can also be given: same, full\nor valid.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe values. Every input sends.\n\nkernel:\nThe weights, held.\n\ndim (int box, also an inlet):\nThe dim the kernel slides along; -1, the last, by default. For frames x joints, use 0.\n\nmode:\nAn option, under the node. same (the default) keeps the input's length; full includes every position where the kernel overlaps the input at all (length + kernel - 1); valid only the positions where it overlaps completely.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nconvolved:\nThe result.\n\nThe answer stays on the input's device: a tensor on the GPU is worked on there and comes out there.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTwo spikes, convolved with the kernel 1 2 1: each spike becomes a copy of the kernel.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.convolve is the numpy version. t.correlate finds where a pattern occurs. scipy.filtfilt and the Butterworth filters smooth by frequency. scipy.gaussian_filter blurs."
        },
